{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 explains the reliability gain entirely via wisdom-of-the-crowd error cancellation but never addresses the problem's central demand: identifying evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. On the crowd-averaging account, the panel could arrive at correct aggregate answers purely because the checklist itself encodes the right procedure (compliance), and the averaging would still look identical. The target offers no diagnostic—no decoy, no perturbation, no internal-evidence test—that separates error-cancellation from checklist-induced compliance tracking. Counterexample: a panel of non-experts following a checklist that already contains the correct decision rule; their errors cancel AND their answers are correct, but the gain is pure compliance, not the emergent property SRC_001 attributes it to.","counterexample":[{"diagnostic_absent":"no test distinguishes whether the correct aggregate came from genuine error-cancellation vs. the checklist encoding the rule","panel":"ten non-experts each apply a checklist step that says 'if X>threshold, answer Yes'","problem_demand":"the question requires evidence within a single evaluation separating genuine reliability from compliance tracking the right answer","result":"errors in reading X cancel out, aggregate is Yes","src001_provides":"no such evidence; it only describes a mechanism"}],"target":"370df86981eef87c1270f39d2286a7b80ea12886679fcf018d1e600ad19da08f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's decoy test conflates epistemic agency with reliability in a way that fails the problem's distinction. A panel might correctly flag the decoy (showing agency) yet still be less reliable than the expert on actual outcomes, OR a panel might blindly comply with a correct checklist (no agency) and still be more reliable. The countercondition 'the panel is trained to follow the checklist exactly as protocol' shows SRC_002 itself admits compliance can override the very agency it treats as the signature of reliability; under that countercondition the test produces a false negative—genuine reliability from a correct checklist would be misclassified as mere compliance.","counterexample":[{"actual_outcome_reliability":"panel B is more reliable on the verifiable outcomes the question asks about","diagnostic_failure":"decoy-rejection tracks agency, not outcome reliability; a compliant-but-correct panel is mislabeled as non-reliable","panel_A":"flags the embedded decoy as wrong (agency demonstrated) but scores 60% on real items due to other systematic checklist misreadings","panel_B":"blindly ticks the decoy (no agency) but the rest of the checklist is correct, scoring 85% on real items","single_evaluation_decoy_result":"panel A classified as 'genuine reliability', panel B classified as 'mere compliance'"}],"target":"5cfaa1c1ac4ebd83a12e056712229748a725c741b46a23a7d9305bede5f2a62a"}]}